{
  "core_question": "What analyses must the hospital board conduct across clinical, legal, and financial domains to evaluate a private‑equity acquisition involving conversion to for‑profit status, sale‑leaseback of real estate, and a charitable foundation holding proceeds?",
  "rationale": "The transaction touches patient care standards (clinical), nonprofit tax status and contractual structures (legal), and valuation, financing, and cash‑flow projections (finance).",
  "routes": ["healthcare", "legal", "finance"],
  "sub_questions": {
    "healthcare": "What clinical considerations—including patient safety, workforce retention, accreditation requirements, and community benefit obligations—must the board assess when converting a rural nonprofit hospital to for‑profit status and entering a sale‑leaseback arrangement?",
    "legal": "From a legal standpoint, what are the key regulatory, contractual, and fiduciary issues the board must address in (1) converting the hospital’s nonprofit entity to a for‑profit corporation, (2) structuring the sale‑leaseback of the real estate, and (3) establishing a charitable foundation that holds the proceeds?",
    "finance": "What financial analyses—including valuation of the operating entity, leaseback economics, debt service coverage, cash‑flow projections, tax implications, and expected return on investment—should the board perform to evaluate the feasibility and attractiveness of this private‑equity acquisition?"
  },
  "requires_recency_disclosure": true,
  "recency_notes": "Current IRS rules on sale‑leaseback for hospitals, state hospital licensing requirements, and recent private‑equity acquisition guidelines may have been updated after the specialists’ training cutoffs."
